  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 153 PM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nA decrease in mid-level shortwave activity is anticipated by\nFriday afternoon, so a gradual decrease in the intensity of rain\nshould follow. However, we continue to see adequate moisture along\nwith an upper level jet moving in from the west, so rain chances\nwill continue out west and spread eastward across the remainder of\nsouth central Texas. The highest rainfall amounts for Thursday and\nFriday still remain focused across the southern Edwards Plateau\nand Rio Grande plains region, especially Val Verde and Edwards\nCounties, where rainfall amounts of 1 to 2 inches with isolated\namounts up to 4 inches are possible. Amounts will taper\nsignificantly as you head east into the I-35 corridor, where\naround 1/2\" are forecast. \n\nOn Saturday, some lingering moisture and instability will be\npresent over most of the region, but rain chances should generally\nremain favored mainly north of U.S. Highway 90. With the loss of\ndaytime heating, rain chances drop out of the forecast Saturday\nevening and overnight. Mid-level ridging builds over the area\nSunday in advance of a deepening upper trough over the western\nU.S., which should yield dry weather for most of the region. \n\nThe next weather system to impact the region will be dropping\nsouthward into the southern U.S. plains states early next week. We\ndo continue to see some timing differences between the operational\nGFS and ECMWF and for now will show an increase in rain chances\nfor most of the region beginning Monday night with a cold front\nexpected to move through on Tuesday. Both of the above mentioned\nmodels do show a line of convection with the passage of the cold\nfront, so another opportunity for rainfall exists for areas likely\nto miss out on the Thursday and Friday rainfall event. The front \nis expected to drop temperatures to near or slightly below normal \nfor Tuesday into Wednesday.",
